  "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =9(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
